|~ What is declared under `::map`: the refusal of its constructor, and
   the verbs of maps, each with the parts of its object and its answer.
   A map's elements are its values, and
   its keys are the shape that travels with them: a verb that keeps the
   kind keeps the keys of the entries it keeps. ~|

:map/construct
  |~~ Reads a map as the bare map, and builds one from the vector of its pairs `[key value]`. ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:map/PayloadNotMapError
  |~~ `::map` reads a map as the map it is and a vector as its pairs;
      a payload of another kind is refused.
      ~(
        ::map(1)
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::map/PayloadNotMapError ::number]
       )
  ~~|

:map/ElementNotPairError
  |~~ Each element of the vector `::map` builds from is a pair `[key
      value]`; `:index` names the first that is not.
      ~(
        [[:a 1] [:b]] | tag ::map
        !| [type /index]
        |  eq [::map/ElementNotPairError 1]
       )
  ~~|

:map/KeyNotKeywordError
  |~~ The key of a pair is a keyword, or a string or a number naming the
      keyword of its text; `:index` names the pair whose key is another
      value.
      ~(
        [[[1] 2]] | tag ::map
        !| [type /actualType /index]
        |  eq [::map/KeyNotKeywordError ::vec 0]
       )
  ~~|

:map/count
  |~~ The number of entries.

      ~({:x 1 :y 2 :z 3} | count | eq 3)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:map/count/size
  |~~ How many entries the map holds. ~~|
  ::number

:map/empty
  |~~ Whether the map holds no entry.

      ~({} | empty | eq true)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:map/empty/vacant
  |~~ Whether the map holds no entry. ~~|
  ::boolean

:map/first
  |~~ The value of the first entry, or null for the empty map.

      ~({:a 1 :b 2} | first | eq 1)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:map/last
  |~~ The value of the last entry, or null for the empty map.

      ~({:a 1 :b 2} | last | eq 2)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:map/sum
  |~~ The sum of the values, each a number.

      ~({:a 10 :b 20} | sum | eq 30)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:map/sum/total
  |~~ The sum of the values. ~~|
  ::number

:map/avg
  |~~ The mean of the values, each a number, or null when there is none.

      ~({:a 1 :b 3} | avg | eq 2)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:map/avg/mean
  |~~ The mean of the values, null for an empty map. ~~|
  #[::number ::null]

:map/min
  |~~ The first value in the one order of values, or the one whose key
      answers the first value; the key of the entry is read by `entries`.

      ~({:a 3 :b 1} | min | eq 1)
      ~({:a 3 :b 1} | entries | min ~(/1) | /0 | eq :b)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:map/min/key
  |~~ The key the call gives, null when it gives none. ~~|
  ~(at 0)

:map/min/key/NotQuoteOrNullError
  |~~ The key is code, a quote or null. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::quote ::null)

:map/max
  |~~ The last value in the one order of values, or the one whose key
      answers the last value; the key of the entry is read by `entries`.

      ~({:a 3 :b 1} | max | eq 3)
      ~({:a 3 :b 1} | entries | max ~(/1) | /0 | eq :a)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:map/max/key
  |~~ The key the call gives, null when it gives none. ~~|
  ~(at 0)

:map/max/key/NotQuoteOrNullError
  |~~ The key is code, a quote or null. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::quote ::null)

:map/filter
  |~~ The entries whose value the predicate answers true for, their keys
      kept; the joint test of a key with its value reads the keys, `m |
      keys | filter ~(…)`, and `at` the value of each.

      ~({:a 1 :b 2 :c 3} | filter ~(gt 1) | eq {:b 2 :c 3})
      ~({} | filter ~(gt 0) | eq {})
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:map/filter/predicate
  |~~ The code each element is run through, which answers a boolean. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:map/filter/predicate/NotQuoteError
  |~~ The predicate is code, a quote. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::quote)

:map/filter/kept
  |~~ The entries whose values the predicate accepted. ~~|
  ::map

:map/every
  |~~ Whether the predicate answers true for every value.

      ~({:a 1 :b 2 :c 3} | every ~(gt 0) | eq true)
      ~({:a 1 :b 2} | every ~(gt 0) | eq true)
      A map is asked of its values:
      ~({:a 1 :b -2 :c 3} | every ~(gt 0) | eq false)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:map/every/predicate
  |~~ The code each element is run through, which answers a boolean. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:map/every/predicate/NotQuoteError
  |~~ The predicate is code, a quote. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::quote)

:map/every/all
  |~~ Whether the predicate held for every value. ~~|
  ::boolean

:map/any
  |~~ Whether the predicate answers true for some value.

      ~({:a -1 :b 0 :c 2} | any ~(gt 0) | eq true)
      ~({:a -1 :b 2} | any ~(gt 0) | eq true)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:map/any/predicate
  |~~ The code each element is run through, which answers a boolean. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:map/any/predicate/NotQuoteError
  |~~ The predicate is code, a quote. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::quote)

:map/any/some
  |~~ Whether the predicate held for some value. ~~|
  ::boolean

:map/sort
  |~~ The entries ordered by their values, or by the value the key
      answers for each, their keys kept.

      ~({:a 3 :b 1 :c 2} | sort | vals | eq [1 2 3])
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:map/sort/key
  |~~ The key the call gives, null when it gives none. ~~|
  ~(at 0)

:map/sort/key/NotQuoteOrNullError
  |~~ The key is code, a quote or null. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::quote ::null)

:map/sort/sorted
  |~~ The entries in the order the key ranks their values. ~~|
  ::map

:map/take
  |~~ The first count entries.

      ~({:a 1 :b 2 :c 3} | take 2 | eq {:a 1 :b 2})
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:map/take/count
  |~~ How many. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:map/take/count/NotNumberError
  |~~ The count is a number. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::number)

:map/take/count/NotIntegerError
  |~~ The count is a whole number. ~~|
  ::holds~(eq (round))

:map/take/taken
  |~~ The first entries. ~~|
  ::map

:map/drop
  |~~ The entries after the first count, in the order of the map; a
      key leaves a map by `minus`, which takes the keys to leave out.

      ~({:a 1 :b 2 :c 3} | drop 2 | eq {:c 3})
      ~({:a 1 :b 2 :c 3} | minus #[:b] | eq {:a 1 :c 3})
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:map/drop/count
  |~~ How many. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:map/drop/count/NotNumberError
  |~~ The count is a number. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::number)

:map/drop/count/NotIntegerError
  |~~ The count is a whole number. ~~|
  ::holds~(eq (round))

:map/drop/rest
  |~~ The entries after the ones dropped. ~~|
  ::map

:map/at
  |~~ The value at the key, a keyword or a string, both naming the key
      as the map stores it, or null for a key the map lacks: the soft
      counterpart of the strict `/key` projection, which refuses a
      miss with `::proj/KeyNotInMapError`.

      ~({:a 1 :b 2} | at :a | eq 1)
      ~({:a 1 :b 2} | at "a" | eq 1)
      ~({:a 1} | at :z | eq null)
      ~({:a 1} | at :z 0 | eq 0)
      ~({:a null} | at :a | at :b | eq null)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:map/at/key
  |~~ The key the call gives.

      The key of `at` on a map is a keyword or a string, both naming the
      key as the map stores it: `at :email`. A modifier is computed
      against the subject before `at` runs, so `at /email` reads the
      value of the field first and hands that value to `at` as the key;
      `:actualValue` holds what it handed.

      ~(
        {:a 1} | at 42
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::map/at/key/NotKeywordOrStringError ::number]
       )
      ~({:email {:home "a@b"}} | at /email !| /actualValue | eq {:home "a@b"})
      ~({:email {:home "a@b"}} | at :email | eq {:home "a@b"})
  ~~|
  ~(/0)

:map/at/key/NotKeywordOrStringError
  |~~ The key is a keyword or a string. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::keyword ::string)

:map/at/default
  |~~ The value answered for a place the subject does not hold, null when it gives none. ~~|
  ~(at 1)

:map/reverse
  |~~ The entries in the reverse order, their keys kept.

      ~({:a 1 :b 2} | reverse | vals | eq [2 1])
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:map/reverse/reversed
  |~~ The entries in reverse order. ~~|
  ::map

:map/reduce
  |~~ The seed folded over the values by the reducer.

      ~({:a 1 :b 2} | reduce 0 ~(add) | eq 3)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:map/reduce/reducer
  |~~ The code that folds an element into the value so far. ~~|
  ~(/1)

:map/reduce/reducer/NotQuoteError
  |~~ The reducer is code, a quote. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::quote)

:map/reduce/seed
  |~~ The value the fold starts from. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:map/keys
  |~~ The set of the keys, keywords in the one order: sorted by name.

      ~({:name "Alice" :age 30} | keys | eq #[:age :name])
      ~({:name "Alice" :age 30} | keys | first | eq :age)

      `keys` answers the keys of a map; a vector of maps answers them
      element by element, `* keys`, and a vector's own places are its
      indices, `/0`.
      ~(
        42 | keys
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::map/keys/SubjectNotMapError ::number]
       )
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:map/keys/keySet
  |~~ The keys of the map. ~~|
  ::set

:map/vals
  |~~ The vector of the values, in the order of the entries.

      ~({:name "Alice" :age 30} | vals | eq ["Alice" 30])

      `vals` answers the values of a map; a vector of maps answers them
      element by element, `* vals`.
      ~(
        [1 2] | vals
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::map/vals/SubjectNotMapError ::vec]
       )
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:map/vals/values
  |~~ The values of the map, in order. ~~|
  ::vec

:map/entries
  |~~ The vector of the pairs `[key value]` of the map, in the order of
      the entries, each key a keyword: the map with its keys as data, for
      a step that reads a key beside its value, as `[type payload]` reads
      a tagged value. `tag ::map` builds the map back.

      ~({:auth {:port 3000} :db {:port 5432}} | entries | first | eq [:auth {:port 3000}])
      ~({:auth {:port 3000}} | entries * (unionOf /1 {:name (/0 | keyword)}) | eq [{:port 3000 :name "auth"}])
      ~({:axios "^1.6.0"} | entries * ([(/0 | keyword) "@" /1] | join "") | eq ["axios@^1.6.0"])
      ~({:a 1 :tmp 2} | entries | filter ~(/0 | ne :tmp) | tag ::map | eq {:a 1})

      `entries` answers the pairs of a map; a vector of maps answers
      them element by element, `* entries`.
      ~(
        [1 2] | entries
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::map/entries/SubjectNotMapError ::vec]
       )
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:map/entries/pairs
  |~~ The pairs of key and value, in order. ~~|
  ::vec

:map/has
  |~~ Whether the map holds the key, a keyword or a string, both naming
      the key as the map stores it, so the `keys | first | has _` chain
      composes.

      ~({:name "Alice"} | has :name | eq true)
      ~({:name "Alice"} | has "name" | eq true)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:map/has/key
  |~~ The key the call gives.

      The key of `has` on a map is a keyword or a string, both naming
      the key as the map stores it.
      ~(
        {:a 1} | has 42
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::map/has/key/NotKeywordOrStringError ::number]
       )
  ~~|
  ~(/0)

:map/has/key/NotKeywordOrStringError
  |~~ The key is a keyword or a string. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::keyword ::string)

:map/has/held
  |~~ Whether the map holds the key. ~~|
  ::boolean

:map/union
  |~~ The entries of both maps, the other map's value winning a key both
      hold.

      ~({:a 1 :b 2} | union {:b 3 :c 4} | eq {:a 1 :b 3 :c 4})
      ~({:a 1} | union {:b 2} | eq {:a 1 :b 2})
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:map/union/with
  |~~ The value the subject is joined with. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:map/union/with/NotMapError
  |~~ The with is a map. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::map)

:map/union/merged
  |~~ The entries of both maps, the second one's value winning a key both hold. ~~|
  ::map

:map/minus
  |~~ The entries whose keys the other operand lacks: a map's keys, or the
      keys a vector or a set names, each a keyword or a string, as `at`
      reads a key.

      ~({:a 1 :b 2 :tmp 3} | minus #[:tmp] | eq {:a 1 :b 2})
      ~({:a 1 :b 2} | minus ["a"] | eq {:b 2})
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:map/minus/keys
  |~~ The keys the call gives. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:map/minus/keys/NotMapOrVecError
  |~~ The keys is a map or a vector. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::map ::vec)

:map/minus/remaining
  |~~ The entries whose keys the other operand does not hold. ~~|
  ::map

:map/inter
  |~~ The entries whose keys the other operand holds: a map's keys, or
      the keys a vector or a set names, each a keyword or a string.

      ~({:a 1 :b 2 :c 3} | inter #[:a :b] | eq {:a 1 :b 2})
      ~({:a 1 :b 2 :c 3} | inter ["a" :c] | eq {:a 1 :c 3})
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:map/inter/keys
  |~~ The keys the call gives. ~~|
  ~(/0)

:map/inter/keys/NotMapOrVecError
  |~~ The keys is a map or a vector. ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::map ::vec)

:map/inter/common
  |~~ The entries whose keys the other operand holds. ~~|
  ::map

:map/MinusKeyNotKeywordOrStringError
  |~~ A key `minus` takes out of a map is a keyword or a string; `:index`
      names the first element of the other operand that is neither.
      ~(
        {:a 1} | minus [:a 1]
        !| [type /actualType /index]
        |  eq [::map/MinusKeyNotKeywordOrStringError ::number 1]
       )
  ~~|

:map/InterKeyNotKeywordOrStringError
  |~~ A key `inter` keeps of a map is a keyword or a string; `:index`
      names the first element of the other operand that is neither.
      ~(
        {:a 1} | inter [[:a]]
        !| [type /actualType /index]
        |  eq [::map/InterKeyNotKeywordOrStringError ::vec 0]
       )
  ~~|

:map/raise
  |~~ Puts the map on the fail track as an error, the act the `!{…}`
      literal writes with its bang, so a query fails where its own check
      fails. `|` and `*` step around the raised error into its trail, and
      `!|` opens it to the error itself.

      `map | raise` raises the subject, a map the step builds among them:
      ~({:kind :oops :message "boom"} | raise !| /kind | eq :oops)
      ~(42 | {:kind :oops :value /} | raise !| /value | eq 42)
      Its tag is the one its value shows, the outermost tag over the
      map, then the one the map carries, then a `:kind` that names a
      tag, then `::error`, so an opened error raised again is itself:
      ~("x" | add 1 !| raise !| type | eq ::number/add/SubjectNotNumberError)
      A step that lays a tag over an opened error renames it:
      ~("x" | add 1 !| tag ::Renamed | raise !| type | eq ::Renamed)
      A guard raises an error of its own tag when a value fails its
      check:
      ~(42 | if (gt 100) ~(/) ~({:kind ::TooSmall :value /} | raise) !| [type /value] | eq [::TooSmall 42])

      `raise` puts a map on the fail track.

      ~(
        42 | raise
        !| [type /actualType]
        |  eq [::map/raise/SubjectNotMapError ::number]
       )
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}
